WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.730
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:11.120
The 2016 Rio Olympic Games is right around
the corner, so it is the perfect time to talk

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.980
a bit about how we can use science to predict
the results.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.630
Before we start, I'd like to mention that
we won't be showcasing any predictions for

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:24.940
this year's Olympics, instead, we are going
to talk about a model that was used to predict

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.770
the results of previous Olympic Games events.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:33.160
So, the following, very simple question arises:
can we predict the future?

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.660
The answer is very simple: no, we can't.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.090
End of video, thanks for watching!

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:44.880
Well, jokes aside, we cannot predict the future
itself, but we can predict what is likely

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.510
to happen based on our experience of what
happened so far.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:52.130
In mathematics, this is what we call extrapolation.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:57.829
There is also a big difference between trying
to extrapolate the results of one athlete,

00:00:57.829 --> 00:01:03.829
or the aggregated number of medals for many
athletes, usually an entire nation.

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:09.220
To bring up an example about traffic, if we
were to predict where one individual car is

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:12.610
heading, we would obviously fail most of the
time.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:17.650
However, whichever city we live in, we know
exactly the hotspots where there are traffic

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.600
jams every single morning of the year.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:25.560
We cannot accurately predict the behavior
of one individual, but if we increase the

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.490
size of the problem and predict for a group
of people, it suddenly gets easier.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:37.659
Going back to the Olympics, will Usain Bolt
win the gold on the 100 meter sprint this

00:01:37.659 --> 00:01:38.659
year?

00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:43.939
Predicting the results of one athlete is usually
hopeless, and we bravely call such endeavors

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:45.479
to be mere speculation.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:50.920
The guy whose results we're trying to predict
may not even show up this year - as many of

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.920
you have heard, many of the Russian athletes
have been banned from the Olympic Games.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.530
Our model would sure as hell not be able to
predict this.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:00.530
Or would it?

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:05.640
We'll see in a second, but hopefully it is
easy to see that macro-level predictions are

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:09.380
much more feasible than predicting on an individual
level.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:16.290
In fact, to demonstrate how much of an understatement
it is to say feasible, hold onto your seatbelts,

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:23.400
because Daniel Johnson, a professor of microeconomics
at the Colorado College created a simple prediction

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:30.790
model, that, over the past 5 Olympic Games,
was able to achieve 94% agreement between

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:34.400
the predicted and actual medal counts per
nation.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.120
What is even more amazing is that the model
doesn't even take into consideration the athletic

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.210
abilities of any of these contenders.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:44.489
Wow!

00:02:44.489 --> 00:02:50.920
Media articles report that his model uses
only 5 simple variables: a country's per-capita

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:57.569
income, population, political structure, climate,
and host-nation advantage.

00:02:57.569 --> 00:03:03.069
Now first, I'd first like to mention that
GDP per capita means the Gross Domestic Product

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:08.459
of one person in a given country, therefore
it is independent of the population of the

00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:09.540
country.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.379
If we sit down and read the paper, which is
a great and very easy read and you should

00:03:14.379 --> 00:03:17.569
definitely have a look, it's in the video
description box.

00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:23.769
So, upon reading the paper, we realize there
are more variables that are subject to scrutiny:

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:29.350
for instance, a proximity factor, which encodes
the distance from the hosting nation.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:35.379
Not only the hosting nation itself, but its
neighbors are also enjoying significant advantages

00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:40.819
in the form of lower transportation costs
and being used to the climate of the venue.

00:03:40.819 --> 00:03:45.620
Unfortunately I haven't found his predictions
for this year's Olympics, but based on the

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.489
simplicity of the model, it should be quite
easy to run the predictions provided that

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:52.569
the sufficient data is available.

00:03:52.569 --> 00:03:57.470
The take home message is that usually the
bigger the group we're trying to predict results

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:02.340
for, the lesser the number of variables that
are enough to explain their behavior.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:08.190
If we are talking about the Olympics, 5 or
6 variables are enough to faithfully predict

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.349
nationwide medal counts.

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:15.650
These are amazing results that are also a
nice testament to the power of mathematics.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:21.979
I also really like how the citation count
of the paper gets a big bump every four years.

00:04:21.979 --> 00:04:24.349
I wonder why?

00:04:24.349 --> 00:04:29.360
If you are interested in how the Olympic Games
unfold, make sure to have a look at the Olympics

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.779
reddit, I found it to be second to none.

00:04:31.779 --> 00:04:35.280
As always, the link is available in the description
box.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.300
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

